~\<private-repo>\06_math_research\probability_1000_intake_claude_20260907\attempts.jsonl:3:{"attempt_id": "C0003-A001", "author": "claude-probability-1000-continue-0907", "independent_check": "", "key_identity": "", "method": "Try to derive the tail exponent from the Gibbs-Markov fixed-point equation as for smoothing transforms (Durrett-Liggett classification).", "obstruction": "Smoothing-transform theory fixes the power in the tail but not the logarithmic correction; the log factor is what (5) asserts.", "problem_id": "C0003", "recorded_at": "2026-09-07T06:38:45Z", "result": "The Gibbs-Markov property along dyadic square partitions gives a fixed-point equation of smoothing type Z^D =_d sum_i e^{alpha Phi_i} Z^{D_i} with Gaussian binding field Phi and 4 pieces; Durrett-Liggett gives, for such equations, that non-trivial solutions have tails whose exponent is the root of a structure equation, here forcing E[Z^D(A)^s] finite iff s < 1. This yields the correct tail EXPONENT (P(Z^D(A) > t) decays like t^{-1} up to slowly varying factors) but not the precise Laplace asymptotic E e^{-lambda Z} = 1 - c lambda log(1/lambda)(1+o(1)) demanded by property (5); the slowly varying factor is exactly the content of the conjecture and the Durrett-Liggett theory does not fix it.", "schema": "probability-attempt.v1", "substantive": true}
~\<private-repo>\06_math_research\probability_1000_intake_claude_20260907\attempts.jsonl:29:{"attempt_id": "C0036-A001", "author": "claude-probability-1000-continue-0907", "correction_note": "Codex review 10:53: the key identity conflated the Schur complement s with the probability s/(1+s).", "independent_check": "Schur-complement monotonicity: (M_S^{-1})_{ee} is nondecreasing in S for positive definite M (Cauchy interlacing / Schur complement); the domination criterion is the classical one-site condition for product measures.", "key_identity": "min over B of P_Q(e in X | X minus e = B) = 1/(J^{-1})_{ee} = HM(spec Q) for equivariant Q on a finite group; hence P_Q >= Bernoulli(HM(Q)) rigorously, versus the conjectured Bernoulli(GM(Q)).", "method": "Prove a weaker rigorous bound via one-site conditional probabilities and test the conjectured bound exactly on cyclic groups by monotone-coupling linear programs.", "obstruction": "Closing the gap between harmonic and geometric mean requires using conditional probabilities given partial information, not the worst-case conditioning.", "problem_id": "C0036", "recorded_at": "2026-09-07T06:38:45Z", "result": "Rigorous partial result. For a finite group Gamma and an equivariant Q with 0 < Q < I, P_Q(X = A) = det(I-Q) det(J_A) with J = Q(I-Q)^{-1}, so P(e in X | X minus e = B) = s_B/(1+s_B) with s_B the Schur complement J_ee - J_{eB} J_B^{-1} J_{Be} = 1/((J_{B union e})^{-1})_{ee}, which is decreasing in B; its minimum over B is 1/(J^{-1})_{ee} = 1/((Q^{-1})_{ee} - 1). Equivariance gives (Q^{-1})_{ee} = (1/|Gamma|) sum over characters of 1/lambda_chi = 1/HM where HM is the harmonic mean of the spectrum, so every one-site conditional probability of presence is at least HM. By Holley's inequality specialised to a product measure (its condition reduces to the one-site statement that every conditional probability of presence given the rest is at least p), P_Q dominates the product measure of density HM(Q), and symmetrically is dominated by the product measure of density 1 - HM(I-Q). Since HM <= GM = FK, this is weaker than the conjecture, and the conjecture's content is exactly the improvement from harmonic to geometric mean. Numerical test of the conjecture itself: on Z/nZ, n = 3..6, 102 random symmetric circulant contractions, the monotone-coupling LP (minimum violation) certifies BOTH conjectured dominations with zero violation in every instance (raw/C_lyons_conj57_fk_domination_check.json); optimality check on one n = 5 instance: raising the product density from FK to FK + 0.01 already makes the lower domination infeasible (violation 0.006), so the FK bound is sharp to within 0.01 there.", "schema": "probability-attempt.v1", "substantive": true, "superseded_by": "C0036-A002"}
~\<private-repo>\06_math_research\probability_1000_intake_claude_20260907\attempts.jsonl:49:{"attempt_id": "C0060-A001", "author": "claude-probability-1000-continue-0907", "independent_check": "", "key_identity": "Capacity of a box of side L in G x Z is about L^{0.26}; the gasket's cut points force vacant crossings through corner regions whose hitting probability tends to 1.", "method": "Try the renormalisation argument for u* > 0 with the gasket's polynomial growth and heat-kernel bounds.", "obstruction": "The Z-direction bypass of gasket cut points is not quantified; the sign of the answer is unclear.", "problem_id": "C0060", "recorded_at": "2026-09-07T06:38:45Z", "result": "The Z^d proof of u* > 0 (Sznitman) uses a renormalisation over boxes at scales L_n with the decoupling of the interlacement in distant boxes controlled by the capacity of boxes (order L^{d-2}) against the volume of the sprinkling; on G x Z the walk has spectral dimension d_s = 2 log 3 / log 5 + 1 = 2.365 and walk dimension d_w = log 5 / log 2 = 2.32 in G, and the capacity of a box of side L in G x Z is of order L^{d_f + 1 - d_w} with d_f = log 3/log 2 = 1.585, i.e. L^{0.26}: capacity grows very slowly. The renormalisation needs the number of boxes at scale L_{n+1} covering a box at scale L_n times the probability that interlacement at level u hits a box (about u cap(box)) to be summable; with capacity L^{0.26} and the gasket's self-similar structure one needs the box at scale L_n to be avoided by the vacant-set-crossing with probability decaying faster than the branching of the renormalisation, which fails because the exponent 0.26 is too small to dominate the combinatorial factor of the gasket's cut sets: a crossing of a gasket triangle can be blocked by hitting one of only three corner regions (the gasket has cut points), so the vacant set is blocked whenever the interlacement hits the three corner neighbourhoods, which at level u happens with probability roughly 1 - exp(-3u cap(corner region)) -> a constant not tending to zero as the scale grows only if cap(corner region) stays bounded; since cap grows like L^{0.26} it tends to infinity and the blocking probability tends to 1. This argues for u* = 0 on G x Z: at any u > 0, at large scales every corner region is hit and the gasket's cut-point structure disconnects the vacant set. The argument is heuristic because the corner regions of the Z-direction are cylinders, not points, and a vacant path can bypass a corner through the Z direction; the honest conclusion is that the answer hinges on whether the Z direction provides bypasses of the gasket's cut points, which I could not settle.", "schema": "probability-attempt.v1", "substantive": true}
~\<private-repo>\06_math_research\probability_1000_intake_claude_20260907\attempts.jsonl:73:{"attempt_id": "C0085-A001", "author": "claude-probability-1000-continue-0907", "independent_check": "", "key_identity": "", "method": "Compare the invasion cluster two-point function with the IIC two-point function and locate the difference.", "obstruction": "Requires slightly supercritical two-point asymptotics with constants (C0065-type input).", "problem_id": "C0085", "recorded_at": "2026-09-07T06:52:37Z", "result": "The IIC two-point function P_IIC(x in cluster) is asymptotic to A' |x|^{-(d-4)} in high dimensions (from the lace expansion of the IIC, Hofstad-Jarai), and the invasion percolation cluster is expected to have the same decay exponent because the invasion cluster near infinity looks like a critical cluster with a slightly supercritical 'ponds' structure at scales below the current pond size. The source notes that the IIC and IPC have different scaling limits on the tree (Angel-Goodman-den Hollander-Slade), so the constants A and A' differ and the exponent may agree. A rigorous bound: since the invasion cluster contains the IIC-like structure... no inclusion holds in either direction. The ponds structure: invasion percolation on Z^d invades the cluster of the origin at level p_c + eps_k where eps_k decreases to 0 along the pond sequence; the probability that x at distance r is invaded is roughly the probability that x lies in the p_c + eps(r) cluster of the origin restricted to the ball of radius r, with eps(r) the level at scale r; in high dimensions eps(r) ~ r^{-2} (mean-field correlation length exponent 1/2), and P_{p_c + r^{-2}}(0 <-> x, |x| = r) is of order r^{-(d-2)} times the number of extra connections... a computation via the slightly supercritical two-point function gives P(x in IPC) ~ r^{-(d-2)} x (number of vertices at distance about r in the cluster)/... I could not make this precise; the mean-field prediction of the exponent d-4 comes from the IIC analogy. Not resolved; the reduction is to a slightly supercritical two-point function estimate at p_c + r^{-2} with matching constants, which is a Problem 11.1-type supercritical quantity.", "schema": "probability-attempt.v1", "substantive": true}
~\<private-repo>\06_math_research\probability_1000_intake_claude_20260907\attempts.jsonl:107:{"attempt_id": "C0131-A001", "author": "claude-probability-1000-continue-0907", "independent_check": "", "key_identity": "", "method": "Compare with the RWRE 0-1 law in d >= 3 (C0044) and locate the extra difficulty from self-interaction.", "obstruction": "Self-interaction breaks the coupling arguments behind Markovian 0-1 laws.", "problem_id": "C0131", "recorded_at": "2026-09-07T07:09:31Z", "result": "For Markovian RWRE the dichotomy question is the directional 0-1 law (C0044, open in d >= 3). For excited random walks the process is not Markov, and even the statement 'either recurrent or transient' (return to the origin infinitely often a.s. or finitely often a.s.) is not implied by any Markov-chain dichotomy; the source's Definition 3.1 asks for a 0-1 law for the number of returns. A route: the event {X returns to 0 infinitely often} is determined by the walk in the environment, and by the ergodicity of the environment its probability does not depend on the starting point in the sense P_x(return to x i.o.) is constant in x; but P_0(return to 0 i.o.) in (0,1) is not excluded by that. The Kalikow-type argument for RWRE (P_0(A_l union A_{-l}) in {0,1}) uses that the walk from 0 and from a far point in the same environment can be coupled after a hitting event; for excited walks the coupled walks see different cookie states (one walk has eaten cookies), which is the extra difficulty. Under (UEL) and boundedness of the stacks (M cookies per site), a walk that has visited a region changes the environment there in a bounded way, and once the walk leaves a region for good the modified cookies are irrelevant; so for transient behaviour the modification is harmless, while recurrence requires infinitely many returns through modified regions. I could not produce a proof; the sharpest statement I can defend: if the excited walk is transient in the sense |X_n| -> infinity with positive probability, then, under (IID) and (UEL), it is transient a.s., by the following argument: on the event of transience the walk eventually leaves every ball, and the environment outside any ball is untouched and i.i.d., so the probability of transience given the walk is at distance R from the origin at a time after which it never returns to the ball... this does not close either because the walk's future depends on the eaten cookies inside the ball only if it returns. Recorded as attempted without result.", "schema": "probability-attempt.v1", "substantive": true}
~\<private-repo>\06_math_research\probability_1000_intake_claude_20260907\attempts.jsonl:118:{"attempt_id": "C0036-A002", "author": "claude-probability-1000-continue-0907", "independent_check": "Correction checked line by line against the source and the holder's message by the Fable agent; the holder's claim was taken as a lead, not as authority.", "key_identity": "min_B P(e in X | X minus e = B) = 1/(Q^{-1})_ee = HM(spec Q); the Schur complement s_min = HM/(1-HM) is the odds, not the probability.", "method": "Codex review 10:53: the key identity conflated the Schur complement s with the probability s/(1+s).", "obstruction": "see A001 for the standing obstruction", "problem_id": "C0036", "recorded_at": "2026-09-07T09:27:17Z", "result": "CORRECTION of C0036-A001's key_identity line only. Correct chain: s_B = det J_{B+e}/det J_B is nonincreasing in B; s_min = 1/(J^{-1})_ee = 1/((Q^{-1})_ee - 1) = HM/(1 - HM); the minimal conditional probability of presence is s_min/(1 + s_min) = 1/(Q^{-1})_ee = HM(Q). The A001 key_identity wrote 'min P(e | rest) = 1/(J^{-1})_ee = HM', which equates the odds with the probability; the proof text in the math-brain node (harmonic-mean lemma) already has the correct chain. Conclusion (domination by Bernoulli(HM)) unchanged.", "schema": "probability-attempt.v1", "substantive": true, "supersedes": "C0036-A001"}
